==Trivia==
* In Tragic Determination, Mitsuhide has a special quote when fighting Nobunaga, where he says ''"Our enemy...is in <kingdom's name>!"''. This is likely a reference to the real-life {{wp|Honnō-ji Incident}}, where {{wp|Akechi Mitsuhide}} led a group of {{wp|Oda Nobunaga}}'s own soldiers against him, famously declaring that ''"The enemy is at Honnō-ji!"''.
** Ironically, Tragic Determination tends to place Nobunaga in kingdoms adjacent to Mitsuhide's kingdoms, so this interaction is likely to trigger early in the episode - which goes counter to its tone of finality.
* Mitsuhide and [[Hideyoshi]] have special quotes when battling each other in a kingdom with banners. Their Pokémon also get maximum energy when the quotes are shown. This is probably a reference to the {{wp|Battle of Yamazaki}}, after {{wp|Akechi Mitsuhide}} betrayed and killed {{wp|Oda Nobunaga}}.
* In the alpha build of Pokémon Conquest, there existed [https://tcrf.net/File:PSL_Alpha_Title_Pokemon_Conquest.png an image] of Mitsuhide and his {{p|Moltres}} fighting [[Hero]] and his {{p|Eevee}}. Moltres does not exist in the final version of Conquest, though the graphic can still be found in the game files.

===Historical origin===
Mitsuhide is based on the real-life {{wp|Akechi Mitsuhide}} of Japanese history, best known for his betrayal of [[Nobunaga]]. His wife was [[Hiroko]] ({{jwp|妻木煕子|Tsumaki Hiroko}}); he had at least three daughters with her, one of them being [[Gracia]], and another one married [[Hidemitsu]] ({{wp|Akechi Hidemitsu}}), his cousin. [[Mitsutada]] ({{wp|Akechi Mitsutada}}) was his cousin, too. [[Toshimitsu]] ({{wp|Saitō Toshimitsu}}), once a retainer for [[Ittetsu]] ({{wp|Inaba Yoshimichi}}), defected to Mitsuhide to serve as his retainer. It is unknown if Mitsuhide died in the aftermath of the {{wp|Battle of Yamazaki}}, and he is rumored to have become {{wp|Tenkai}} (a Buddhist monk), who served [[Ieyasu]]. However, the game treats Mitsuhide and [[Tenkai]] as two separate characters.

Mitsuhide's specialty in Ice, which is the only type supereffective against Dragon aside from Dragon itself, may be a reference to his betrayal of Nobunaga.

